<span class="qd-heading"><b>About Quadriga-Lib</b></span>
<br><br>

<p><b>Quadriga-Lib</b> is an open-source C++ library with MATLAB, Octave and Python interfaces for radio channel modelling and simulation.
It generates channel impulse responses for system-level studies of mobile radio networks. It also provides tools for array antennas,
channel data storage and processing, and site-specific (ray-tracing) simulations.</p><br>

<b>Who it is for</b>

<ul>
<li><b>Researchers and engineers</b> who need realistic radio channels for link- and system-level simulations,
antenna array studies or ray-tracing post-processing. Install it with <code>pip install quadriga-lib</code> and try the
<a href="#quickstart">example below</a>.<br><br></li>
<li><b>QuaDRiGa users.</b> Quadriga-Lib is the C++ successor of the
<a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://quadriga-channel-model.de">QuaDRiGa</a> MATLAB channel model, by the same lead developer.
It is not a drop-in replacement: functions, names and file formats follow their own API.
<a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://github.com/stjaeckel/QuaDRiGa-NG">QuaDRiGa-NG</a> is a fork of the
MATLAB toolbox that uses Quadriga-Lib in its backend.<br><br></li>
<li><b>Standardization work.</b> The library implements the 3GPP TR 38.901 stochastic channel model and the IEEE 802.11 indoor
channel models (TGn, TGac, TGax, TGah). The <a href="publications.html">publications</a> describe the implementation and
compare the two model families.</li>
</ul>

<br>
<b>Features</b>

<ul>
<li><b>Stochastic channel model (GBSM)</b>: large-scale and small-scale fading parameters, path loss, LOS probability, O2I loss, and scenario parameters for 3GPP TR 38.901</li>
<li><b>IEEE 802.11 (Wi-Fi) indoor channel models</b>: TGn channel types A to F with the TGac, TGax and TGah extensions (MU-MIMO, floor penetration loss, time-evolving channels)</li>
<li><b>Spatially consistent random fields</b>: sum-of-sinusoids generators for correlated random variables</li>
<li><b>Array antennas</b>: predefined models (omni, dipole, ULA, 3GPP panels, multibeam), pattern interpolation, rotation, coupling, directivity and beamwidth, QDANT file format</li>
<li><b>Channel coefficients</b>: antenna patterns applied to path data (spherical and planar wave, multi-frequency, IRS), baseband frequency response, delay quantization, delay and angular spread, K-factor, XPR</li>
<li><b>Channel data storage</b>: HDF5 channel files for large datasets (read, write, reshape)</li>
<li><b>Site-specific simulation</b>: ray–triangle and ray–point intersection, mesh tools, OBJ and Mitsuba XML export, reading of QRT ray-tracing results</li>
<li><b>Acoustics (experimental)</b>: room-acoustic simulation with the ray tracer, loudspeaker models and acoustic material tables; under active development</li>
<li><b>Fast core</b>: C++17 with OpenMP, optional AVX2 and CUDA acceleration</li>
</ul>

<br>
